#### 6. Magic Number Test
**Ref:** testsmells.org — Magic Number Test
**Test:** `CalculateTotal_ReturnsCorrectAmount` (line 69)

```csharp
// ❌ SMELL — where do 247.50, 22.28, 269.78 come from?
Assert.AreEqual(247.50m, order.TotalAmount);
Assert.AreEqual(22.28m, order.TaxAmount);
Assert.AreEqual(269.78m, order.GrandTotal);
```

**Fix — use named constants documenting the derivation:**
```csharp
// SKU-1 = $49.99 × 3 = $149.97; SKU-2 = $97.53 × 1 = $97.53
const decimal expectedSubtotal = 149.97m + 97.53m;       // 247.50
const decimal expectedTax      = expectedSubtotal * 0.09m; // 22.28 (9% tax)
const decimal expectedGrand    = expectedSubtotal + expectedTax;

Assert.AreEqual(expectedSubtotal, order.TotalAmount);
Assert.AreEqual(expectedTax, order.TaxAmount);
Assert.AreEqual(expectedGrand, order.GrandTotal);
```
**Risk:** Numbers are unverifiable without reading production code — reviewer cannot confirm correctness.

